---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import RotateVideo from '@/components/tools/media/RotateVideo.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="flip-video">
  <RotateVideo client:load mode="flip" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Mirror, not rotate</h2>
    <p>
      A flip reflects the frame across an axis. A horizontal flip swaps left and right, like a bathroom
      mirror; a vertical flip swaps top and bottom. A rotation turns the whole frame around its centre and
      never reverses anything. People often confuse the two, and the wrong choice gives a video that is
      upside down and backwards.
    </p>
    <p>
      A quick test: if writing in the shot reads correctly and must stay that way, you want
      <a href="/rotate-video">rotation</a>. If writing in the shot reads backwards, you want a horizontal
      flip.
    </p>

    <h2>The webcam mirroring problem</h2>
    <p>
      Video-call software shows your own camera feed mirrored, because a preview that is not mirrored feels
      wrong: you move your left hand and the person on screen moves their right. Most apps mirror only the
      preview and record the true image, but some record what you saw. Then all logos, labels and
      whiteboards in the shot are reversed.
    </p>
    <p>
      A horizontal flip fixes this in one pass. It is also correct when a recording was made through a
      mirror or a prism-based rig, or when the front camera of a phone saved the mirrored version.
    </p>

    <h2>Where each direction is useful</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Flip</th>
            <th>What it does</th>
            <th>Typical reason</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Horizontal</td>
            <td>Left and right swap</td>
            <td>Un-mirroring a selfie or webcam clip; reversing text; making a dance or exercise tutorial follow along on the correct side</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Vertical</td>
            <td>Top and bottom swap</td>
            <td>Footage from an inverted camera mount, a microscope, a telescope or a ceiling-mounted action camera</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Both</td>
            <td>Identical to a 180° rotation</td>
            <td>Convenience: two flips and one rotation give the same frame</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Why flipping needs a re-encode</h2>
    <p>
      Containers have a metadata flag for rotation ("turn this 90 degrees") but none for mirroring. So the
      only way to flip a video is to decode each frame, reverse the pixel order and encode it again. The
      mirror itself is lossless (no pixel value changes), but the H.264 compression runs a second time.
    </p>
    <p>
      At the default quality, the second pass is not visible. If the clip goes into an editor for more work,
      choose the high quality preset in the advanced options, because each re-encode adds loss.
    </p>

    <h2>Check it before you export</h2>
    <p>
      The preview above applies the flip live with CSS, so you can play the clip and confirm the result
      before you spend time on encoding. The exported file uses the FFmpeg <code>hflip</code> and
      <code>vflip</code> filters, the same operations that desktop editors use internally.
    </p>

    <h2>Local, unwatermarked, unlimited</h2>
    <p>
      The flip video tool processes the video in this tab, and nothing is transmitted. The export has no
      watermark, and there is no limit on clip length and no account.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
